WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.600
A signal this strong has to be kept off most of the time.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.380
First brake: the ligand is stored locked.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.140
TGF-β is secreted still wrapped in the front part of its precursor chain.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.940
It stays bound and covers the surfaces receptors need.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.200
It is usually tethered to the extracellular matrix.

6
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.080
For example, a cell's integrins can pull on the wrapper, or a protease can cut it.

7
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.540
Active ligand is released locally.

8
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:37.453
Second brake: the signal makes its own inhibitor.

9
00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:42.953
One of the genes that Smads switch on encodes Smad7.

10
00:00:43.953 --> 00:00:49.833
Smad7 binds the active type I receptor, and keeps the receptor's Smads away.

11
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.133
That is negative feedback.

12
00:00:52.983 --> 00:00:56.263
Third brake: the parts are destroyed.

13
00:00:57.343 --> 00:01:01.923
Smad7 brings ubiquitin ligases called Smurfs to the receptor.

14
00:01:02.883 --> 00:01:07.723
Smurfs tag the receptor, and some Smads, with ubiquitin for destruction.

15
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:12.277
Scott Kern's laboratory found Smad4.

16
00:01:12.857 --> 00:01:16.257
It is inactivated in about half of pancreatic cancers.

17
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:23.537
Mice made without the myostatin gene, in Se-Jin Lee's laboratory, have muscles two

18
00:01:23.537 --> 00:01:25.417
to three times the normal weight.

19
00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:32.597
In fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva, muscle and connective tissue turn to bone.

20
00:01:32.597 --> 00:01:38.637
Nearly all patients with the classic form carry the same one amino acid change.

21
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:43.297
It sits in the GS domain of a BMP type I receptor.
